 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Current limiting",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Keep each LED's current within its absolute maximum and always put a resistor in its path: the forward characteristic is steep, so a small change in voltage moves the current a lot and can destroy the part. A resistor per LED (circuit A) holds each LED's current; LEDs sharing one resistor at a fixed voltage (circuit B) split the current unevenly with their forward-voltage spread."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Heat",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Self-heating and ambient temperature change the LED: hotter means lower efficiency, less light and a shift in colour, so the system design has to account for the LED's heat."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Hand soldering",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Hand soldering is for repair and rework only: an iron under 30 W held below 300 ℃, no more than 3 s per terminal, once per terminal."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Reflow soldering",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Reflow no more than twice. Put no stress on the LED while it heats, and leave it alone until it has cooled to room temperature. The preferred peak is 240±5 ℃ for 6 s."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Clean with alcohol: up to 3 minutes at 30 ℃ or less, or up to 30 s at 50 ℃ or less. Any other solvent must first be shown not to attack the package or resin. Ultrasonic cleaning is possible at generally no more than 300 W, after a trial to check for damage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Storage and baking",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Shipped in a sealed moisture-barrier, antistatic bag with desiccant. Sealed, store at 30 ℃ or less and under 60% RH and use within one year. Once opened, store at 30 ℃ or less and under 40% RH and solder within 168 hours (7 days); the working area should be 30 ℃ or less and under 60% RH. If the desiccant has failed or the storage limits were exceeded, bake unsoldered parts at (60±5) ℃ for 24 hours."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Static electricity",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "Electrostatic discharge and surges damage the LED; a damaged part may show a lower forward voltage, fail to light at low current, or not light at all. Ground all equipment and handle the parts with wrist straps or antistatic gloves and containers."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Do not touch the encapsulant with bare fingers, which soils it and changes its optics, and do not press on it, least of all when hot during reflow: force can crack the die or bend the bond wire. The epoxy scratches easily; when using tweezers, do not grip the resin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Eye safety",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Looking straight into a lit LED, above all a bright one, can harm the eyes."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Tape orientation and leader",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "On the reel the cathode side of each part faces the sprocket-hole edge of the carrier tape. The tape starts with at least 160 mm of cover tape alone or cover tape over empty pockets, then at least 320 mm of cover tape over empty pockets, then the loaded pockets, and ends with at least 360 mm of cover tape over empty pockets."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Packing and label",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Reels are packed in a moisture-proof antistatic foil bag with desiccant, and bags in a cardboard box. The reel label carries CPN (batch and bin), MPN (model), LOT (date and encapsulant colour), WL (wavelength code), IV (brightness code), VF (voltage code), package, origin and quantity."
  }
 ],
